// RaceInPast -- the paper's Example 2, races bounded in the past. Once r0
// reads the flag, both writes of a happen before both reads, and CoWR keeps
// each read from seeing a write that the other write follows in co. So the two
// reads agree. Java's model lets them differ.
//
// The OCaml memory model (Dolan, Sivaramakrishnan & Madhavapeddy, PLDI 2018,
// section 6). An access in mode sc is to an atomic location, any other access
// non-atomic.
a := 0;
flag := 0;
{
  a := 1;
  flag.store(1, sc)
} ||| {
  a := 2;
  r0 := flag.load(sc);
  r1 := a;
  r2 := a
}
%%
forbid (r0 = 1 && r1 = 1 && r2 = 2) [OCaml]
forbid (r0 = 1 && r1 = 2 && r2 = 1) [OCaml]
forbid (r0 = 1 && r1 = 2 && r2 = 1) [RC11]
forbid (r0 = 1 && r1 = 2 && r2 = 1) [smrd]
